{
  "Version": 2,
  "Name": "Gerken2003-optimized-66layer",
  "Source": "M. Gerken and D. A. B. Miller, \"Multilayer thin-film structures with high spatial dispersion,\" Appl. Opt. 42, 1330-1345 (2003). doi:10.1364/AO.42.001330 - Appendix D, Table 1, 'Section 6' columns (layers 1-66); Sec. 6 text p. 1340; Figs. 9, 10",
  "Notes": "Numerically optimized nonperiodic SiO2/Ta2O5 stack with a linear spatial shift over ~20 nm around 830 nm (combined penetration-depth and stored-energy dispersion); fabricated and measured\nSpecification: Linearly increasing spatial shift for 20 wavelengths over a 20-nm range around 830 nm, 54 deg, p polarization; impedance matched; layer thickness 57-454 nm\nReported: Total thickness 13.4 um (table sum 13378.5 nm); min layer 57 nm, max 453 nm (text says 454). Fig. 9(b) (with back gold layer, read from plot): shift ~2 um at 821 nm, ~7.5 um at 828 nm, ~15.5 um at 835 nm, ~24 um at 842 nm. Fig. 10 (no gold, measured and calculated): linear shift 820-840 nm, ~0 at 818 to ~21 um at 842 nm.\nNotes: Same order and medium convention as the four-cavity design: Table 1 layer 1 (Ta2O5) is next to the quartz substrate, through which light is incident; kept as layer 1. Exit medium: Fig. 9 calculation includes a back-side gold coating ('reflectance improved to nearly 100 % by a gold layer on the extreme right', thickness and index not given); Fig. 10 calculation and the measurement setup comparison omit it - air is used here. angle_deg = 54 in air; ~32.2 deg inside quartz. Indices assumed constant 2.06 / 1.456.\nLayers from Appendix D, Table 1, 'Section 6' columns (layers 1-66); Sec. 6 text p. 1340; Figs. 9, 10; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), agrees with the paper: ours total 13378.5 nm; paper table sum 13378.5 nm (paper: 13.4 um).",
